<phyphox version="1.20" locale="en">
  <title>Strobe Tachometer</title>
  <category>Data centre</category>
  <icon>STB</icon>
  <description>
        WHAT IT DOES
        Measures fan speed with your eyes instead of with maths.

        HOW
        A strobe is a light flashed fast, on and off, over and over. Here the phone torch is the strobe. It flashes at a rate you choose. Point it at the fan and slide the rate until the blades appear to stop dead. When they look frozen, the fan is turning exactly once between flashes. So the speed is simply the flash rate times 60, which gives the turns per minute (RPM).

        WHY IT IS WORTH HAVING
        Nothing is being worked out or guessed. You can SEE it working. That makes it the honest check on the Fan Tacho, which works out speed from sound. The Fan Tacho can be fooled by a harmonic (a whole-number multiple of a tone, so twice the pitch, three times, and so on) or by the fan next door. If the two disagree, believe this one.

        Two methods that work by completely different physics, agreeing, is real confidence. One method agreeing with itself is not.

        THE TRAP
        Blades also look frozen at half the speed, a third, a quarter, and so on. So always find the HIGHEST flash rate that freezes them. That is the real one.

        THE CEILING
        Phones cannot flash faster than about 40 times a second. That caps direct reading at 2400 RPM. A faster fan will still freeze, but at a fraction of its real speed. Use the flashes per revolution box to correct for that. Flashes per revolution means how many times the light flashed during one full turn of the fan.

        It also will not work in bright light. A phone torch cannot outshine hall lighting. Use it in a dim aisle, or shade the fan.

        SAFETY - READ IT
        A strobe makes moving machinery look stopped. People have put their hands into machines that looked still. Treat anything you have strobed as running, always. Never use this as your reason to believe something has stopped. Isolate it properly.

        Flashing light can also trigger seizures in people with photosensitive epilepsy, a condition where flashing light can bring on a seizure. This range of flash rates is the most likely to do it. Warn anyone nearby, and do not use it where people cannot look away.

        NEEDS PHYPHOX 1.2.1 OR NEWER. The flashlight feature only arrives in that version, which is still a public test version (a beta). On 1.2.0 and earlier, phyphox will refuse to open this file and say 'Unknown tag flashlight'. That is a version gap, not a fault.
    </description>

  <data-containers>
    <container init="25">strobeHz</container>
    <container init="1">intensity</container>
    <container init="0.1">duty</container>

    <container>rpm1</container>
    <container>rpm2</container>
    <container>rpmHalf</container>
    <container>rpmThird</container>
    <container init="1">divisor</container>
    <container>rpmTrue</container>
  </data-containers>

  <output>
    <flashlight>
      <input parameter="intensity">intensity</input>
      <input parameter="frequency">strobeHz</input>
      <input parameter="dutycycle">duty</input>
    </flashlight>
  </output>

  <views>
    <view label="Strobe">
      <info label="Slide until the blades freeze. Start high and come down slowly - the first place it freezes as you come down is the real speed."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <slider label="Flash rate" type="normal" default="25" minValue="1" maxValue="40" stepSize="0.05" precision="2" showValue="true">
        <output>strobeHz</output>
      </slider>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="Fan speed if frozen at this rate" unit="RPM" size="3" precision="0">
        <input>rpm1</input>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="If you had to go below the fan's real speed to freeze it, divide the true rate here and read the corrected figure."/>
      <edit label="Flashes per revolution (flashes during one full turn)" default="1" decimal="false">
        <output>divisor</output>
      </edit>
      <value label="Corrected speed" unit="RPM" size="2" precision="0">
        <input>rpmTrue</input>
      </value>
    </view>

    <view label="Is it really frozen">
      <info label="A strobe freezes the image at the true speed AND at every whole fraction of it. These are the other speeds that would look identical at the current flash rate."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="If one flash per turn" unit="RPM" precision="0">
        <input>rpm1</input>
      </value>
      <value label="If it is really turning twice as fast" unit="RPM" precision="0">
        <input>rpm2</input>
      </value>
      <value label="If it is really half this" unit="RPM" precision="0">
        <input>rpmHalf</input>
      </value>
      <value label="If it is really a third of this" unit="RPM" precision="0">
        <input>rpmThird</input>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="How to tell them apart: at the true speed a fan shows its normal number of blades, standing still. At half the true speed you see twice as many blades as the fan has. Count the blades in the frozen image and compare with the real fan. That settles it every time."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="Slower is safer for finding the fundamental, which is the real base speed. Start at 40 and walk the slider down. The FIRST rate that gives a clean single frozen image is the fundamental. Rates below that give doubled and tripled images."/>
    </view>

    <view label="Torch settings">
      <slider label="Brightness" type="normal" default="1" minValue="0.05" maxValue="1" stepSize="0.05" precision="2" showValue="true">
        <output>intensity</output>
      </slider>
      <slider label="Flash length" type="normal" default="0.1" minValue="0.02" maxValue="0.5" stepSize="0.01" precision="2" showValue="true">
        <output>duty</output>
      </slider>
      <info label="Shorter flashes freeze the image more sharply but look dimmer. If the blades are blurry rather than frozen, shorten the flash before anything else."/>
    </view>

    <view label="Notes">
      <info label="Read this before pointing it at a machine"/>
      <info label="A strobe at the right rate makes a spinning fan look completely still. People have put their hands into machines that looked stopped. Treat anything you have strobed as running, always. Never use this to confirm something is off. Isolate it properly instead."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="Flashing lights can trigger seizures in people with photosensitive epilepsy, a condition where flashing light can bring on a seizure. The danger is strongest between about 5 and 30 flashes per second, which is exactly this range. Do not use it where people cannot avoid seeing it, and warn anyone nearby first."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="The 40 Hz ceiling"/>
      <info label="Most phones cannot switch the flash faster than about once every 25 milliseconds (thousandths of a second), so roughly 40 Hz is the real limit. That reads up to 2400 RPM directly. A 6000 RPM fan will not freeze at any rate on the slider. But it will freeze at 3000 RPM worth of flashing, which is 25 Hz, showing two images. Use the flashes-per-revolution box to correct it."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="In bright light this will not work at all. A phone torch cannot compete with hall lighting. It works best in a dim aisle, or with the fan shaded."/>
    </view>
  </views>

  <analysis sleep="0.2">
    <multiply>
      <input as="factor" clear="false">strobeHz</input>
      <input as="factor" type="value">60</input>
      <output clear="false">rpm1</output>
    </multiply>
    <multiply>
      <input as="factor" clear="false">rpm1</input>
      <input as="factor" type="value">2</input>
      <output>rpm2</output>
    </multiply>
    <divide>
      <input clear="false">rpm1</input>
      <input type="value">2</input>
      <output>rpmHalf</output>
    </divide>
    <divide>
      <input clear="false">rpm1</input>
      <input type="value">3</input>
      <output>rpmThird</output>
    </divide>
    <formula formula="[1]/max([2],1)">
      <input clear="false">rpm1</input>
      <input clear="false">divisor</input>
      <output>rpmTrue</output>
    </formula>
  </analysis>

  <export>
    <set name="Reading">
      <data name="Flash rate (Hz)">strobeHz</data>
      <data name="Speed at one flash per turn (RPM)">rpm1</data>
      <data name="Flashes per revolution">divisor</data>
      <data name="Corrected speed (RPM)">rpmTrue</data>
    </set>
  </export>
</phyphox>
